Image: [Wikimedia Commons](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pluto_and_Charon_(4092391173).jpg)

Looking for programming languages I found two that are interesting,
mainly because they allow using the pipe operator `|>` like **Elixir**.

## Pluto

_Pluto_ is a programming language based on _Lua_. It is a fork of the Lua VM
with some additional things that are very good.

```lua
local { http, json } = require "pluto:*"

http.request("https://httpbin.org/get")
|> json.decode
|> dumpvar
|> print
```

<https://pluto-lang.org>

## Roc

Roc is a functional and immutable language.

<https://www.roc-lang.org/>

```elixir
app [main] { pf: platform "https://github.com/roc-lang/basic-cli/releases/download/0.10.0/vNe6s9hWzoTZtFmNkvEICPErI9ptji_ySjicO6CkucY.tar.br" }

import pf.Stdout
import pf.Task

main =
    List.range { start: At 1, end: At 100 }
    |> List.map fizzBuzz
    |> Str.joinWith ","
    |> Stdout.line
```

## Wren

An experiment was also made in _Wren_ to use _Pipes_ although,
since it is a slower-paced language, nothing official exists yet.

<https://github.com/wren-lang/wren/issues/1144>

One comment by [**@mhermier**](https://github.com/wren-lang/wren/issues/1144#issuecomment-1489819677) stands out:

Object Oriented Programming (OOP) is not about creating
a giant taxonomy with a complex hierarchy of objects.

The excessive use of dot syntax (`object.method()`) is a
consequence of that misinterpretation.

OOP is about having objects that quack like a duck, but
are not necessarily a duck (quack like a duck, but is not a duck).

There is great confusion about the idea that in OOP
only methods exist and not functions. Even _Smalltalk_ (the Father of OOP) has functions
(hidden under a special syntax).

[...]

Pipes are a great tool for working with functions and
should be considered both for functions and for objects
that act as functions (following the principles of OOP).
